WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.869
Picture paying for a coffee from the money you keep in a café chain's app, your wallet.

2
00:00:07.869 --> 00:00:12.207
The app says it went through, and your wallet drops from 100.00 to 70.00.

3
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.783
That evening, the café's owner checks what she's owed.

4
00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:17.196
Your 30.00 isn't there.

5
00:00:17.196 --> 00:00:19.591
It left your wallet, and never arrived.

6
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.184
How can money leave one place and never land in the other?

7
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:26.216
And how do two machines agree to do both, or neither?

8
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:32.978
The app keeps its records in a database, a program that stores rows of data, a bit like a spreadsheet.

9
00:00:32.978 --> 00:00:37.233
The wallets live on one database server, a computer called wallets.

10
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:40.536
The shops' balances live on another, called payouts.

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.627
On one server, this is easy.

12
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:47.063
A transaction groups changes so that all of them happen, or none do.

13
00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:53.129
It commits, becomes permanent, at one moment: when one line reaches the server's log on disk.

14
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.364
But our payment has two halves, on two disks.

15
00:00:56.364 --> 00:00:58.405
No single line can commit both.

16
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:03.351
So the obvious plan: commit the debit on wallets, then the credit on payouts.

17
00:01:03.351 --> 00:01:06.211
Now payouts crashes a moment before its commit.

18
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:09.264
You've paid 30.00, and the café got nothing.

19
00:01:09.264 --> 00:01:10.853
That's our missing money.

20
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.463
Then undo the debit?

21
00:01:12.463 --> 00:01:17.451
By then, another payment may already have been approved against your 70.00.

22
00:01:17.451 --> 00:01:22.106
Putting it back is a new transaction, a refund, and that can fail too.

23
00:01:22.106 --> 00:01:22.571
Well...

24
00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:24.500
we've just moved the problem.

25
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:29.744
What we want is this: nobody commits until everybody has promised that they can.

26
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.475
Picture organising a dinner by text.

27
00:01:32.475 --> 00:01:34.985
You ask each friend: Friday at eight?

28
00:01:34.985 --> 00:01:37.563
If you say yes, keep the evening free.

29
00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:44.129
Only when everyone has said yes do you write dinner is on in your diary, and then text everyone.

30
00:01:44.129 --> 00:01:46.249
If anyone says no, you text off.

31
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:47.314
Let's map it.

32
00:01:47.314 --> 00:01:52.724
You're the coordinator: the program running the payment, which decides how it ends.

33
00:01:52.724 --> 00:01:56.961
The friends are the participants: the servers that hold the data.

34
00:01:56.961 --> 00:02:00.872
And the whole thing gets one id that everyone shares, txf-1.

35
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:02.548
Phase one is prepare.

36
00:02:02.548 --> 00:02:07.397
The coordinator sends each server its changes, and asks: can you commit?

37
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:15.607
wallets checks its rules, writes the changes and a line saying prepared to its log, makes sure the disk has them, and answers yes.

38
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:17.212
That yes is a promise.

39
00:02:17.212 --> 00:02:21.923
Whatever happens next, even a crash and a restart, it will commit if told to.

40
00:02:21.923 --> 00:02:29.569
So it can't give up on its own any more, and it keeps a lock on your wallet's row, a mark that stops anyone else changing it.

41
00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:31.893
That's the friend keeping Friday free.

42
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.560
payouts does the same, and says yes.

43
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:37.932
Both servers said yes, and neither has committed.

44
00:02:37.932 --> 00:02:41.095
So at what moment is the payment really committed?

45
00:02:41.095 --> 00:02:42.107
Pause and guess.

46
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:51.733
The moment the coordinator writes one line to its own log, txf-1 COMMIT, and the disk has it.

47
00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:54.072
That one line is the commit point.

48
00:02:54.072 --> 00:02:55.999
It's the note in your diary.

49
00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:59.900
Neither server has changed yet, but the outcome is fixed.

50
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.515
Phase two is commit.

51
00:03:01.515 --> 00:03:04.146
The coordinator tells wallets to commit.

52
00:03:04.146 --> 00:03:06.513
It commits, and lets go of the lock.

53
00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:11.283
For a moment, someone reading both servers sees the debit without the credit.

54
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:13.043
The outcome is all or nothing.

55
00:03:13.043 --> 00:03:16.387
It just doesn't show on both servers at the same instant.

56
00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:18.148
Then payouts commits too.

57
00:03:18.148 --> 00:03:27.057
If payouts were out of reach, the coordinator would keep resending, for minutes or days, because once the line is written, the only way is forward.

58
00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.949
That's two-phase commit.

59
00:03:28.949 --> 00:03:32.177
Everyone promises first, then one line decides.

60
00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:34.211
Now a different run.

61
00:03:34.211 --> 00:03:42.081
This time payouts finds a payout entry for txf-1 already there, so one of its rules fails, and it answers no.

62
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:48.844
The coordinator writes nothing at all, and tells wallets to roll back, to undo its prepared changes.

63
00:03:48.844 --> 00:03:50.667
Your wallet stays at 100.00.

64
00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:54.160
A payment with no line in the log counts as aborted.

65
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.619
That's called presumed abort, and it's safe, because nobody can commit without the line.

66
00:03:59.619 --> 00:04:03.156
A server that doesn't answer in time counts as a no, too.

67
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.911
So notice the two points of no return.

68
00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:09.150
A server's yes takes away its own right to abort.

69
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.124
The coordinator's line takes away everyone's.

70
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:13.267
Now the price.

71
00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:19.634
A normal commit is one round trip, a message there and its answer back, plus one wait for the disk.

72
00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:25.165
Two-phase commit needs two round trips to every server, and three disk waits in a row.

73
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:31.655
Between data centres at the two ends of a continent, a round trip takes about 70 milliseconds.

74
00:04:31.655 --> 00:04:35.825
So each payment spends about 140 on the network, instead of 70.

75
00:04:35.825 --> 00:04:39.597
And every prepared server holds its locks the whole time.

76
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:44.345
It's like a friend who can't accept any other plan until you text back.

77
00:04:44.345 --> 00:04:46.315
Hope you don't lose your phone.

78
00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:50.191
What happens when the coordinator dies right after the yeses?

79
00:04:50.191 --> 00:04:50.953
That's next.

80
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.088
So where did the 30.00 go?

81
00:04:53.088 --> 00:04:55.869
Into the gap between two separate commits.

82
00:04:55.869 --> 00:05:01.563
Two-phase commit closes it: every server promises first, and one line on disk decides.

83
00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.235
One quick check.

84
00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:07.890
payouts said yes, then crashed and restarted before phase two.

85
00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.894
The coordinator's log says txf-1 COMMIT.

86
00:05:10.894 --> 00:05:12.471
What must payouts do?

87
00:05:12.471 --> 00:05:13.672
Pause and think.

88
00:05:16.867 --> 00:05:17.353
Commit.

89
00:05:17.353 --> 00:05:22.768
Its yes was a promise that survives a crash, and the line has already decided.
